<!--
  THE AI GATEWAY POLICY. The platform's single model egress, and the only place model traffic is
  shaped.

  Every model call this platform makes arrives here: RagCore holds no Foundry role at all
  (build/infra/identity/managed-identities.json, identity id-synthia-ragcore), so a direct provider
  call would fail to authenticate even if the code existed — and ragcore/tests/architecture/
  test_no_direct_model_call.py asserts that it does not.

      RagCore -> AI Gateway -> model provider          (the only path)
      RagCore -> Azure OpenAI / Foundry                (does not exist, at four independent levels)

  WHY EVERY POLICY BELOW LIVES HERE RATHER THAN IN THE APPLICATION. A budget enforced by the caller
  is a budget the caller can decline to enforce, and a content check applied by the agent loop is one
  a new call site forgets. Metering, budgets, routing, caching and safety are properties of the
  egress, so they are configured at the egress, where there is exactly one of them.

  WHAT THE APPLICATION SUPPLIES, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY LITTLE. Two headers
  (ragcore/src/ragcore/integrations/model/gateway.py): X-Synthia-Organisation, which every policy
  below counts and meters against, and X-Correlation-Id, which joins a metered token to a
  user-visible outcome. It supplies no model name, no deployment name and no provider — naming one
  would make the caller the routing decision-maker and would break FR-OPS-009, which requires that
  changing or adding a provider not change the agent, governance, retrieval or execution logic.

  ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT ADJUSTABLE:

    1. Prove the caller is the platform.                    (managed identity, validate-azure-ad-token)
    2. Require the organisation header.                      (nothing unattributable is metered)
    3. Content safety on the way IN.                         (before a prompt reaches a provider)
    4. Budget and rate limit, per organisation.              (before tokens are spent)
    5. Semantic cache lookup.                                (after safety, so a cache hit is safe)
    6. Route to a provider backend.                          (the platform's only routing decision)
    7. Content safety on the way OUT, then meter, then store.

  STEP 3 BEFORE STEP 5 IS THE SUBTLE ONE. FR-OPS-010 says a cached response MUST NOT bypass the
  checks a fresh response passes. Inbound safety runs before the cache lookup, so a prompt that would
  be blocked is blocked whether or not something similar was answered earlier.

  REPLACEMENTS. {{...}} are API Management named values, resolved at deployment from Key Vault by
  APIM's own managed identity. None of them is a model provider credential; there is no such
  credential anywhere in this platform.
-->
<policies>
  <inbound>

    <!--
      (1) THE CALLER IS THE PLATFORM, PROVED BY MANAGED IDENTITY.

      RagCore presents an Entra token minted by id-synthia-ragcore
      (SYNTHIA_GATEWAY_ENTRA_SCOPE names the audience). There is no subscription key and no API key
      in this path: a key is not tied to a caller, so per-organisation metering would be
      unattributable and a leaked key would be a model budget anybody could spend.
    -->
    <validate-azure-ad-token tenant-id="{{entra-tenant-id}}" header-name="Authorization" failed-validation-httpcode="401" failed-validation-error-message="Unauthorized">
      <audiences>
        <audience>{{ai-gateway-audience}}</audience>
      </audiences>
      <required-claims>
        <claim name="oid" match="any">
          <value>{{ragcore-identity-object-id}}</value>
        </claim>
      </required-claims>
    </validate-azure-ad-token>

    <!--
      (2) NOTHING UNATTRIBUTABLE IS SERVED.

      Every policy after this one counts against the organisation. A call without the header could
      not be metered, budgeted or throttled, so it is refused rather than served unmetered — an
      unmetered call is the one that escapes the budget, and it would escape it silently.

      403 with no detail: the reason names the control.
    -->
    <choose>
      <when condition="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("X-Synthia-Organisation", string.Empty)))">
        <return-response>
          <set-status code="403" reason="Forbidden" />
          <set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
            <value>application/problem+json</value>
          </set-header>
          <set-body>@("{\"type\":\"https://synthia.synoptek.com/problems/unattributable-model-call\",\"title\":\"Unattributable model call\",\"status\":403,\"detail\":\"A model call is metered against an organisation.\"}")</set-body>
        </return-response>
      </when>
    </choose>

    <!--
      (3) CONTENT SAFETY, INBOUND. Bidirectional: this half guards the prompt, and the outbound
      section guards the completion.

      shield-prompt guards against adversarial input, which matters here beyond the usual reason:
      retrieved content and chat text both reach a prompt, and the platform's position is that
      retrieved content is data and never instruction (FR-EXT-017). This is defence in depth for
      that position, not the position itself — the actual defence is structural, since content
      cannot reach a treatment, a role or an outbound destination.

      The content safety backend authenticates by APIM's managed identity, not by a key.
    -->
    <llm-content-safety backend-id="content-safety-backend" shield-prompt="true">
      <categories output-type="EightSeverityLevels">
        <category name="Hate" threshold="4" />
        <category name="Violence" threshold="4" />
        <category name="SelfHarm" threshold="4" />
        <category name="Sexual" threshold="4" />
      </categories>
    </llm-content-safety>

    <!--
      (4) PER-ORGANISATION BUDGET AND RATE LIMIT (FR-OPS-005).

      Counted on the organisation header rather than on a subscription key, because the platform has
      one caller and many organisations: keying on the subscription would give every organisation a
      single shared budget, which is precisely the noisy-neighbour outcome FR-OPS-005 prohibits.

      estimate-prompt-tokens is true so an over-budget prompt is refused before it is sent to a
      provider. Refusing after the call would have already spent the tokens it was refusing to spend.

      A 429 (rate) or 403 (quota) from here is a THROTTLE, not a failure. The application maps it to
      ModelBudgetExceededError and the user is told the request was limited — never that it failed
      (FR-OPS-006).
    -->
    <llm-token-limit
        counter-key="@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("X-Synthia-Organisation", string.Empty))"
        tokens-per-minute="{{organisation-tokens-per-minute}}"
        token-quota="{{organisation-token-quota}}"
        token-quota-period="Daily"
        estimate-prompt-tokens="true"
        remaining-tokens-variable-name="remainingTokens"
        remaining-quota-tokens-variable-name="remainingQuotaTokens" />

    <!--
      (5) SEMANTIC CACHE LOOKUP (FR-OPS-010).

      vary-by the organisation, and this is the most important line in the file. Without it a cached
      completion produced for one organisation's prompt could be served to another — a cross-tenant
      leak through a cache, which no amount of database-level isolation would catch.

      The cache is Redis and Redis is TRANSIENT ONLY in this platform: it is never an authority and
      never a durable record (plan §Platform environment). A cache miss costs a model call; a lost
      cache costs nothing at all.

      Placed AFTER inbound content safety, so a cache hit does not skip a check a fresh response
      would have passed.
    -->
    <llm-semantic-cache-lookup
        score-threshold="0.05"
        embeddings-backend-id="embeddings-backend"
        embeddings-backend-auth="system-assigned"
        ignore-system-messages="true"
        max-message-count="10">
      <vary-by>@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("X-Synthia-Organisation", string.Empty))</vary-by>
    </llm-semantic-cache-lookup>

    <!--
      (6) PROVIDER ROUTING (§13.5, FR-OPS-009). The one routing decision in the platform, made here
      and nowhere else.

      The backend pool is defined in providers.json. Adding or changing a provider is a change to
      that pool and to nothing else: no agent, governance, retrieval or execution code names a
      provider, so none of it moves.

      The backend's own credentials are set to Managed Identity, so the gateway authenticates onward
      to Foundry as itself. build/policy/azure-identity.json, resource `foundry`, names
      AzureKeyCredential, api_key and OPENAI_API_KEY as forbidden configuration for exactly this hop.
    -->
    <set-backend-service backend-id="model-provider-pool" />

  </inbound>

  <backend>
    <forward-request buffer-request-body="true" />
  </backend>

  <outbound>

    <!--
      (7a) CONTENT SAFETY, OUTBOUND. The other half of bidirectional.

      A completion is provider output, and provider output is checked at the boundary before it
      reaches the agent loop (FR-EXT-021). This is the boundary.
    -->
    <llm-content-safety backend-id="content-safety-backend">
      <categories output-type="EightSeverityLevels">
        <category name="Hate" threshold="4" />
        <category name="Violence" threshold="4" />
        <category name="SelfHarm" threshold="4" />
        <category name="Sexual" threshold="4" />
      </categories>
    </llm-content-safety>

    <!--
      (7b) METERING, IN PROVIDER-NEUTRAL UNITS (FR-OPS-008).

      Tokens, dimensioned by organisation and correlation. Provider-neutral is the requirement:
      usage must stay comparable when a provider changes, so nothing here is counted in a
      provider's own billing unit.

      The organisation dimension is what makes per-organisation budgets auditable after the fact
      rather than merely enforced in the moment.
    -->
    <llm-emit-token-metric namespace="Synthia.ModelUsage">
      <dimension name="Organisation" value="@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("X-Synthia-Organisation", string.Empty))" />
      <dimension name="CorrelationId" value="@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault("X-Correlation-Id", string.Empty))" />
      <dimension name="API ID" />
    </llm-emit-token-metric>

    <!--
      (7c) CACHE STORE. Same vary-by as the lookup, for the same reason: a cache partitioned one way
      on write and another on read is a cross-organisation leak with extra steps.
    -->
    <llm-semantic-cache-store duration="{{semantic-cache-seconds}}" />

    <!--
      Provenance on the response, so the application can state where an answer came from. The
      application records it on ModelResponse.served_from_cache; a cached response MUST be
      attributable (FR-OPS-010) and this is what makes it so.
    -->
    <set-header name="X-Synthia-Served-From-Cache" exists-action="override">
      <value>@(context.Variables.ContainsKey("llm-semantic-cache-lookup-hit") ? "true" : "false")</value>
    </set-header>

  </outbound>

  <on-error>
    <!--
      The provider's error does not reach the caller. It can carry endpoint detail, deployment names
      and request content, none of which the application should see or log — and the application
      already has the only distinction it needs, which is the status code: 403/429 is a throttle,
      everything else is a failure.
    -->
    <set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
      <value>application/problem+json</value>
    </set-header>
    <set-body>@("{\"type\":\"https://synthia.synoptek.com/problems/model-egress\",\"title\":\"Model egress error\",\"status\":" + context.Response.StatusCode + "}")</set-body>
  </on-error>
</policies>
